<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Labyrinth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Labyrinth, in Greek mythology, was the maze built by the craftsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daedalus">Daedalus</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> to hold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minotaur">Minotaur</a>. The hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a> killed the monster there and found his way out with the thread given to him by Minos's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>. Ancient writers placed it at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knossos">Knossos</a>, and the complex <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> palace excavated there by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> has often been linked with the legend.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/labyrinth.jpg" alt="Labyrinth" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlMare · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knossos_silver_coin_400bc.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
